=== Psalm 53
--- Psalm 53 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 53
sub: The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God
refs: Psalm 53:1

--- The nature of the atheist
kicker: Psalm 53 · The fool
big: Psalm 53:1 | mark: The fool hath said in his heart, There is no God
p: Psalm 53 opens with one of the clearest and concise views of the nature of the atheist.
p: While it is, in context, about the man who denies God to do evil, lacking fear in Him who he cannot see, and so, unchecked and without fear proceeds to work iniquity, that is ultimately the driving force behind so-called "intellectual atheism" as well. The danger of this delusion leading to reprobation is very real.

--- Given over
kicker: Psalm 53 · The reprobate mind
p: The brute beast of man, ignoring the evidence of God; His wrath revealed from Heaven against the wicked, His mercy upon the faithful, the very creation that proclaims His power and glory, will engage in all manner of evil, justified within his desperately wicked heart, until God gives him up to his vile affections. Searing his conscience, giving him over to a reprobate mind, and he is filled with all unrighteousness.
p: Taken to the logical end, the fool who began by denying the existence of God, will ultimately become rejected utterly if they continue in their error.

--- Without excuse
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 53:1
scripture: Romans 1:19-24 | mark: so that they are without excuse; Professing themselves to be wise, they became fools

--- There is none righteous
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 53:1–3
p: But the general meaning here in Psalm 53 can apply to all men who don’t know or understand the truth. Well before their being made twice-dead. And it is a warning that can still prick at the heart of the lost, while there is yet time.
p: It is Paul’s paraphrase of this Psalm that we use when we give the gospel to people;
scripture: Romans 3:10-12 | mark: There is none righteous, no, not one

--- None that doeth good
kicker: Psalm 53 · Every one of them is gone back
scripture: Psalm 53:2-3 | mark: there is none that doeth good, no, not one
p: This truth, that we are all sinners, is often sufficient to bring a person to at least hear the salvation plan. But first they have to acknowledge that God is.
p: Usually, if a person admits to the reality of God and does not try to excuse away His existence, getting them to admit they are a sinner is easy. They won’t disagree with the scripture when it says Every one of them is gone back: they are altogether become filthy; there is none that doeth good, no, not one.

--- The law written in their hearts
kicker: Application • The atheist
p: But the fool who denies God will balk at that characterization. The atheist will claim that they do not need fear of God to "do good." That they keep the commandment to love their neighbors, and reject that they are sinners.
p: Of course this is delusional. But even on the surface level, they are acknowledging a truth of the God who they reject existing, a morality that comes explicitly from Him, from the law written in our hearts, by the rebuke of the Spirit those who aren’t reprobate, feel.
scripture: Romans 2:14-15 | mark: the work of the law written in their hearts

--- Fish who deny they are wet
kicker: Application • The atheist
p: They are fish who deny they are wet.
p: And their judgment against others, accusing and excusing the sins of other men, betrays their own heart that denies the truth, by accepting God’s work in them, and His judgement.
scripture: Romans 2:1-3 | mark: Therefore thou art inexcusable, O man

--- Before it is too late
kicker: Application • The atheist
p: This internal conflict between their wicked hearts and the conscience of the Spirit will often bring the fool, the "intellectual atheist," out of it. The cognitive dissonance will trigger repentance for many. There are great Christians who were once seduced by the foolishness of their heart into denying His truth. Professing themselves to be wise, they became fools, but in their foolishness, God’s word preached unto them triggers a change of heart, and an acceptance of the reality of God, before it is "too late, reprobate."
p: But for many, they seek to deny the conflict in their hearts, and they seek to cauterize their conscience, and not wishing to retain God in their knowledge, they prefer to believe a lie.

--- In great fear, where no fear was
kicker: Psalm 53 · The workers of iniquity
scripture: Psalm 53:4-5 | mark: There were they in great fear, where no fear was; God hath despised them
p: The reprobate lives in constant fear. They know the truth somewhere in themselves, but have built up strong walls against it, hardening their hearts and ultimately God hardens it completely. They live in constant fear, trapped in a fight or flight mode. And it leads them to do evermore wicked things, as God scatters their bones.

--- Haters of God
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 53:4–5
scripture: Romans 1:29-32 | mark: Being filled with all unrighteousness
p: God hath despised them.

--- Wise as serpents
kicker: Application • The reprobate
p: We may lament, as David does here, wondering Have the workers of iniquity no knowledge? Do they not understand how God is already judging them? We often cannot comprehend the unsaved person’s refusal to see the truth, let alone one given up and given over to a reprobate mind. Don’t they see the sudden destruction they are headed for?
p: But our inability to think like they do shouldn’t keep us from understanding they exist. We are to be wise as serpents, Jesus said. And so, like righteous Lot, we are vexed by their filthy conversation.

--- Even so, come, Lord Jesus
kicker: Psalm 53 · The salvation of Israel
big: Psalm 53:6 | mark: Oh that the salvation of Israel were come out of Zion!
p: The prayer of David that the deliverer come, the day of the Lord, the judgment of God’s wrath upon a wicked world, is the same prayer we have today for the coming again of Christ. Thy Kingdom come. Even so, come, Lord Jesus.
p: He will deal with the tares and the goats and all the fools who have denied God in their hearts. Come quickly, Lord Jesus. Amen

--- Psalm 53 and Psalm 14
kicker: Psalm 53 · A note
p: It should be noted that Psalm 53 directly mirrors Psalm 14, the major difference is the Lord vs God in its language, while the content is close to identical.
p: We can draw application from this, that the LORD is God and vice versa. The foolish idea some propose that there are different Gods in the Old Testament, can be debunked strictly from this minor distinction within the same language and theme.
p: But we can also draw out the importance of understanding the heart and mind of those that not only reject God’s judgment against their wicked deeds, but their delusional heart that denies He even exists, in order to give them excuse for their evil.

