=== Psalm 55
--- Psalm 55 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 55
sub: The betrayal of a familiar friend
refs: Psalm 55:1

--- Hide not thyself
kicker: Psalm 55 · Give ear to my prayer
scripture: Psalm 55:1-2 | mark: hide not thyself from my supplication
p: Psalm 55 is a Psalm of David, but it isn't given to us when or who it is about.
p: We can make a guess from the context that it may have been during the time of Absalom's rebellion, that perhaps the man David considered an equal, a guide and acquaintance, who gave the King sweet counsel, sage advice, and who accompanied David to the Tabernacle to praise God, was the royal advisor, Ahithophel. Ahithophel betrayed David, took up the seditious plans of the King's son, and after his betrayal, and having his advice ignored by Absalom, went home and hanged himself.

--- Ahithophel
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 55:13
big: 2 Samuel 17:23 | mark: and hanged himself
p: Sadly, for David, the number of betrayals and those who met terrible ends for it, are many. While it is debatable whom this particular Psalm is about, what is clearly shown in its verse is the pain, the sting of betrayal, lies against his character, and the resulting death and destruction that came about by these evil works.

--- I will hide mine eyes
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 55:1
p: He opens, as he often does, petitioning God to hear his prayer. To not hide Himself from his supplication. We know the omnipotence and omniscience of God. His understanding is not bound by time or space. There is nothing He doesn't see or hear.
p: But we also know, at times and for His purpose, God turns a blind eye and a deaf ear to those under His judgement or caught in tribulation that serves a larger purpose.
p: God tells the people this, plainly;
big: Isaiah 1:15 | mark: I will hide mine eyes from you

--- A cloud
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 55:1
p: And the people recognize its truth;
big: Lamentations 3:44
p: It can happen. The judgment of the Lord may be upon someone. But it also might simply feel that way in the midst of trouble. We may feel God is absent, His hand removed from our lives, tossed in the turmoil of the moment.
p: It is wise to not assume to know the mind of God, while seeking His involvement in the situation you're in. Like a subject going before the throne, David's petition is formal and proper, and without presumption.
p: That shows a healthy fear of the Lord.

--- They cast iniquity upon me
kicker: Psalm 55 · The voice of the enemy
scripture: Psalm 55:3-5 | mark: for they cast iniquity upon me, and in wrath they hate me
p: David lays out the complaint that has brought him to seek relief from the Lord. There are wicked men engaged in violence, and who cast the blame and cause for their actions on David, by means of slander, justifying their hatred of him and the evil they are doing, by falsely accusing him.

--- Violence and strife in the city
kicker: Psalm 55 · Horror hath overwhelmed me
scripture: Psalm 55:9-11 | mark: I have seen violence and strife in the city
p: The horror of this has brought David to absolute despair. It has overwhelmed him in sorrow and grief.

--- Readily applicable
kicker: Application • Betrayal
p: For modern Christians, living normal lives, in the relative safety of America, the extremes of intrigue and war that afflict a King in ancient Israel could seem to be unrelatable. We may feel far removed in our comfort from the high stakes and bloody acts being described.
p: But spiritually, the truth given us in these moments is readily applicable.
p: We may have been lied about at work, seen a family member turn on us out of the blue, had false assumptions made about our character, and seen the wicked take opportunity to gain for themselves at our expense. Lost and damaged relationships, people we love hurt by it, and the devastating feelings of loss and misery that comes from it all.

--- Wings like a dove
kicker: Psalm 55 · Fly away
scripture: Psalm 55:6-8 | mark: Oh that I had wings like a dove! for then would I fly away, and be at rest
p: We may, like David, wish we had wings to fly away from it all, we may imagine just fleeing it and finding peace in another place, in some other world.

--- Mine equal, my guide
kicker: Psalm 55 · It was not an enemy
scripture: Psalm 55:12-14 | mark: But it was thou, a man mine equal, my guide, and mine acquaintance
p: And then, in the midst of this sudden chaos caused by our enemies, a friend, a brother, a child, suddenly and without cause that you could have ever imagined, turns on you as well.
p: We may be overwhelmed, as David, especially looking at the fallout the betrayal causes. Guilt, hurt and loss, coupled with having people you trusted take up the cause of those against you, just when you need them the most.

--- Let death seize upon them
kicker: Psalm 55 · Vengeance is the Lord's
big: Psalm 55:15
p: David uses this insult upon injury to seek the Lord's help. That hurt is just cause for God to step in and punish them. To not only remove the threat and bring peace, but fiery vengeance on the wicked and the betrayer.
p: Better imprecatory prayers and recognition that vengeance is the Lord's, than bitterness and depression, guilt and wrath.

--- I will call upon God
kicker: Psalm 55 · The Lord shall save me
p: As always, following the pattern of prayer David lays out, as we desire God to get involved in our lives, we leave judgment to Him as we plead our case, our feelings and broken-hearted appeals, we leave judgment and deliverance to a perfect and holy God.
p: And that should lead us to close our prayer in the blessed hope we have placed our trust in. The peace that passes understanding in Christ.
big: Psalm 55:16

--- Evening, and morning, and at noon
kicker: Psalm 55 · He shall hear my voice
scripture: Psalm 55:17-19 | mark: and he shall hear my voice
p: God shall hear us, afflict the enemies, rescue His children, those that have repented to the truth and fear only Him, who call upon His name.

--- Smoother than butter
kicker: Psalm 55 · The betrayer
scripture: Psalm 55:20-21; Psalm 55:23 | mark: The words of his mouth were smoother than butter, but war was in his heart; but I will trust in thee
p: The betrayer, who with feigned words and flattering tongue conspired to do evil, will be destroyed. The covenantbreakers and the mischief-making men will get their rewards. They will be brought low, to the pit of the grave, to the fires of Hell. They will be taken swiftly, while our trust is in the Lord.

--- Cast thy burden upon the Lord
kicker: Psalm 55 · He shall sustain thee
big: Psalm 55:22
p: The same promise quoted by Peter in the New Testament, showing the continuity of God's word.
scripture: 1 Peter 5:6-7 | mark: Casting all your care upon him; for he careth for you
p: What a comfort. What a blessing. Praise God for His faithfulness. Amen

